WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.917
Buffers and arrays require explicit length
tracking.

2
00:00:05.997 --> 00:00:14.595
C arrays do not automatically carry
runtime bounds information into every operation.

3
00:00:14.675 --> 00:00:22.565
A pointer to the first element may look
like enough information to operate on the

4
00:00:22.645 --> 00:00:29.712
data, but safe code also needs to know the
capacity, the current length,

5
00:00:29.792 --> 00:00:32.265
and the intended result.

6
00:00:32.345 --> 00:00:37.409
Array indexes, pointer arithmetic, copy
lengths,

7
00:00:37.489 --> 00:00:42.553
and string terminators must be reviewed
together.

8
00:00:42.633 --> 00:00:48.875
An index that is valid for one array may
not be valid for another.

9
00:00:48.955 --> 00:00:53.195
A byte count may not match an element
count.

10
00:00:53.275 --> 00:01:01.283
A copy that fits the source may still
overflow the destination if the destination

11
00:01:01.363 --> 00:01:03.483
capacity is smaller.

12
00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:11.807
Off-by-one errors are common when code
forgets space for a terminating null byte or

13
00:01:11.887 --> 00:01:18.836
confuses the number of characters with the
number of bytes available.

14
00:01:18.916 --> 00:01:24.686
A string buffer needs room for the data
and the terminator.

15
00:01:24.766 --> 00:01:29.477
Binary buffers may not have a terminator
at all,

16
00:01:29.557 --> 00:01:36.388
so treating all input as a C string can
create incorrect assumptions.

17
00:01:36.468 --> 00:01:44.476
Copy operations should be based on the
destination capacity, the source length,

18
00:01:44.556 --> 00:01:47.618
and the intended output state.

19
00:01:47.698 --> 00:01:54.058
The review question is not only whether a
copy function was used.

20
00:01:54.138 --> 00:01:59.202
It is whether the code knows how much can
be written,

21
00:01:59.282 --> 00:02:07.526
how much should be written, and whether
the resulting buffer is valid for later use.

22
00:02:07.606 --> 00:02:14.555
Avoid assuming that external input will
fit into a fixed-size buffer.

23
00:02:14.635 --> 00:02:22.525
Files, sockets, device data, command-line
arguments, configuration values,

24
00:02:22.605 --> 00:02:30.849
and environment variables may be longer
than expected or shaped differently than a

25
00:02:30.929 --> 00:02:31.989
test case.

26
00:02:32.069 --> 00:02:39.489
Defensive designs check length before
copying and handle oversized input

27
00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:41.100
deliberately.

28
00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:49.188
Prefer designs that keep buffer capacity
and current length together in the same

29
00:02:49.268 --> 00:02:52.566
structure or function contract.

30
00:02:52.646 --> 00:03:01.007
Buffer-related errors should be handled
safely without dumping sensitive memory,

31
00:03:01.087 --> 00:03:05.681
raw internal data, or confusing
diagnostics.

32
00:03:05.761 --> 00:03:11.649
The secure habit is to make size
information part of the API,

33
00:03:11.729 --> 00:03:15.380
not a guess held in the caller's head.
